**Multiple inheritance in Java.**
- **Classes: no.** A class can extend only one class. This avoids the **diamond problem**: if classes B and C both extend A and override a method, and D extends both B and C, it is ambiguous which version D inherits.
- **Interfaces: yes.** A class can implement many interfaces (`class D implements B, C`), and an interface can extend many interfaces. This gives multiple inheritance of *type* and behaviour contracts.
- **Default methods (Java 8):** if two interfaces provide the same default method, the compiler forces the implementing class to override it and may choose one explicitly with `B.super.hello();`.

```java
interface A { default void hi() { System.out.println("A"); } }
interface B { default void hi() { System.out.println("B"); } }
class C implements A, B {
    public void hi() { A.super.hi(); }  // must resolve the conflict
}
```

**Covariant return types** (Java 5+). When overriding a method, the return type may be a **subtype** of the original return type, instead of exactly the same.

```java
class Animal { Animal get() { return new Animal(); } }
class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override Dog get() { return new Dog(); }   // Dog is a subtype of Animal
}
```

Benefit: callers using a `Dog` reference get a `Dog` back without casting. This applies only to **reference types**; primitive return types must match exactly. Parameter types are *not* covariant (a different parameter type is overloading, not overriding).

**Related rule:** an overriding method cannot reduce visibility or throw broader checked exceptions.
